{"edits": [{"finding_ids": ["app/api_errors.py:10:validator"], "search": "MESSAGE_CODES = {
    "field required": "MISSING",
    "value is not a valid integer": "NOT_AN_INTEGER",
    "ensure this value has at least": "TOO_SHORT",
}", "replace": "MESSAGE_CODES = {
    "Field required": "MISSING",
    "Input should be a valid integer": "NOT_AN_INTEGER",
    "String should have at least": "TOO_SHORT",
}", "explanation": "The failing test shows the v1-style message keys no longer match. Pydantic v2 changed its built-in error messages: 'field required' became 'Field required', 'value is not a valid integer' became 'Input should be a valid integer, unable to parse string as an integer', and the min_length message became 'String should have at least N characters' (v1 was 'ensure this value has at least'). With the old keys, every error fell through to the 'INVALID' default. The keys are updated to the v2 message prefixes. The validator migration from the previous attempt (@field_validator with @classmethod) is kept unchanged."}], "notes": "The failure comes from the MESSAGE_CODES lookup, not the validator. Pydantic v2 error messages differ from v1, so the v1 prefixes never matched and every error mapped to 'INVALID'. I changed only the three dictionary keys to the v2 message prefixes and left the rest of the file as it was, including the previous @field_validator/@classmethod migration. Expected results: {'username': ['MISSING']} and {'age': ['NOT_AN_INTEGER']} for the test payload {'age': 'old'}. I have not run the test. Matching on message text is fragile across Pydantic versions. If the exact message wording varies, matching on err['type'] (for example 'missing', 'int_parsing', 'string_too_short') would be more robust, but that is a larger change than this fix needs."}
